Wi-Fi stage 1: ask the controller to enable the WLAN function.

Diagnostic only. This produces no network interface and is not meant to.

The point is to establish whether CONSYS and our WMT transport can bring the
WLAN function up at all, which is the precondition for any attempt at the rest.
Everything above it is unported: WIFI_RAM_CODE_8163 and the whole 802.11 data
path belong to the vendor WLAN driver, which is 67 .c files, 90 headers and
about 169,500 lines written against 3.18, with nothing in mainline for the
MT6625L (mt76 covers newer parts only).

The split matters and is worth recording. WIFI_RAM_CODE is referenced only
inside drv_wlan/ - CFG_FW_FILENAME - so the WLAN driver downloads its own
firmware. WMT's job is powering the chip, downloading the ROM patch, and
turning functions on and off. We already do all of that for Bluetooth.

So the command is the one we already send, with one byte changed:

	wmt_bt_on_cmd[]   = { 0x01, 0x06, 0x02, 0x00, 0x00, 0x01 };
	wmt_wifi_on_cmd[] = { 0x01, 0x06, 0x02, 0x00, 0x03, 0x01 };
	                                             ^^^^
	01     PKT_TYPE_CMD
	06     OPCODE_FUNC_CTRL
	02 00  SDU length: flag byte + parameter byte
	03     WMTDRV_TYPE_WIFI  (Bluetooth is 0)
	01     on

answered by 02 06 01 00 <status>, status 0 for success. Constants from the
vendor's wmt_core.h and wmt_core_func_ctrl_cmd().

No separate power rail is needed. A comment already in this driver records that
wmt_plat's BT and WIFI PALDO calls land on the same refcounted supply, and the
Bluetooth bring-up immediately above has already raised it.

The call is deliberately non-fatal: a rejection logs a warning and Bluetooth
continues unaffected. Same discipline as the r183 VCN33 efuse read - a
diagnostic must not be able to cost a working feature.

diff -urN stpnew/drivers/bluetooth/hci_mt8163_stp.c stpwifi/drivers/bluetooth/hci_mt8163_stp.c
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_mt8163_stp.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_mt8163_stp.c
@@ -170,6 +170,24 @@
 	{ 0x02, 0x08, 0x04, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x01 };
 static const u8 wmt_bt_on_cmd[] = { 0x01, 0x06, 0x02, 0x00, 0x00, 0x01 };
 static const u8 wmt_bt_on_evt[] = { 0x02, 0x06, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00 };
+
+/*
+ * The same WMT FUNC_CTRL opcode, for the WLAN function instead of Bluetooth.
+ *
+ *   01     PKT_TYPE_CMD
+ *   06     OPCODE_FUNC_CTRL
+ *   02 00  SDU length: flag byte + parameter byte
+ *   03     WMTDRV_TYPE_WIFI   (Bluetooth is 0, which is the only difference)
+ *   01     on
+ *
+ * Answered by 02 06 01 00 <status>, status 0 for success.
+ *
+ * No separate power rail is needed: wmt_plat's BT and WIFI PALDO calls land on
+ * the same refcounted supply, which the Bluetooth bring-up above has already
+ * raised.
+ */
+static const u8 wmt_wifi_on_cmd[] = { 0x01, 0x06, 0x02, 0x00, 0x03, 0x01 };
+static const u8 wmt_wifi_on_evt[] = { 0x02, 0x06, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00 };
 /*
  * Stock reads an efuse to pick the VCN33 rail voltage and then calls
  * wmt_set_pmic_voltage(). PMOS hardcodes 3.3 V. Read the value and log it so it
@@ -1233,6 +1251,29 @@
 	if (ret)
 		return dev_err_probe(stp->dev, ret, "WMT Bluetooth enable failed\n");
 
+	/*
+	 * Wi-Fi stage 1, diagnostic only.
+	 *
+	 * This asks the controller whether it will bring the WLAN function up
+	 * at all. It produces no network interface: the firmware
+	 * (WIFI_RAM_CODE_8163) and the whole 802.11 data path belong to the
+	 * WLAN driver, which is ~169,500 lines of out-of-tree vendor code we
+	 * have not ported. What it does establish is whether the CONSYS side
+	 * and our WMT transport can enable the function, which is the first
+	 * thing any port of that driver would need.
+	 *
+	 * Non-fatal on purpose. A rejection here must not cost us Bluetooth.
+	 */
+	ret = mt8163_stp_wmt_xfer(stp, wmt_wifi_on_cmd,
+				  sizeof(wmt_wifi_on_cmd), wmt_wifi_on_evt,
+				  sizeof(wmt_wifi_on_evt));
+	if (ret)
+		dev_warn(stp->dev,
+			 "WMT Wi-Fi function enable rejected (%d); Bluetooth unaffected\n",
+			 ret);
+	else
+		dev_info(stp->dev, "WMT Wi-Fi function enabled\n");
+
 	dev_info(stp->dev, "WMT/STP bootstrap complete\n");
 	return 0;
 }
